{"kind":"request","text":"codex: speed up the cmd/gitseq-mcp test package, which is the slowest gate cost in every review/merge cycle. Hugh asked for this today (2026-09-01) after finding the MCP tests \"super slow\"; he wants a large speedup but explicitly not a strict 10x target, so treat order-of-magnitude as direction, not a gate.\n\nMEASURED BASELINE (this machine, 2026-09-01, local main c3169d83): 86 tests, 85s wall, fully serial — zero t.Parallel calls in the package. The time is not deliberate sleeps: stall-bound tests already inject 50ms deadlines. The cost is real per-test workroom setup — git init + app.Init (keygen, genesis) + N signed durable appends at roughly 170ms per append, each a chain of git subprocesses. The 11s outlier TestWhoamiWarmResidentAtSignedDepthIsSubsecond builds a depth-64 log through signedWorkspace(t, 64) one append at a time.\n\nDIRECTION (two levers, both expected): (a) t.Parallel() on the isolated tests — nearly every test owns its own t.TempDir() repo and httptest server; (b) fixture reuse — build each signed workspace shape once and clone or copy the template repo per test instead of re-signing dozens of appends. Keep the genuinely time-asserting tests (the subsecond-latency measurement and the elapsed-bound stall tests) OUT of the parallel set: under parallel load they go flaky, and their bounds and measured guarantees must not be weakened or removed to buy speed. Moving the pure latency measurement to cmd/gitseq-perf is acceptable if the package keeps an equivalent functional assertion.\n\nCONDITIONS: (1) the package's wall time is materially reduced — report before/after measurements on the same machine at the exact head, from complete runs (an aborted run is not a measurement), with the same 86 tests or an explicit accounting of any test moved; no test deleted, de-scoped, or weakened, and every elapsed-time assertion still runs serially with its bounds unchanged (or relocated to cmd/gitseq-perf with an equivalent functional test left behind). (2) The full Go gates pass at the exact head, run twice to guard against parallelism flakiness. (3) Implement on a request/\u003cslug\u003e worktree with Rests-On per discipline, publish the exact-head artifact for cmd/gitseq-mcp, and request independent review citing this request; the review should specifically check that no timing test can now interleave with parallel tests.","body":{"conditions":"Package wall time materially reduced with before/after measurements from complete runs on one machine at the exact head; no test deleted, de-scoped, or weakened; elapsed-time assertions stay serial with unchanged bounds or move to cmd/gitseq-perf with an equivalent functional test left behind; full Go gates green twice at the exact head; exact-head artifact for cmd/gitseq-mcp published and independent review requested citing this request, checking that timing tests cannot interleave with parallel tests.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}